latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze Input Grid:\n   a. Identify all unique colors in the grid.\n   b. For each color, create a list of all positions where that color appears.\n\n2. Shape Analysis:\n   a. For each color group:\n      i. Determine the bounding box of all positions.\n      ii. Analyze the pattern within the bounding box (vertical, horizontal, block, corner, scattered).\n      iii. Identify key structural elements (e.g., lines, corners, symmetries).\n      iv. Calculate the frequency of different structural elements.\n\n3. Shape Idealization:\n   a. For each color group:\n      i. Based on the pattern and structural elements, generate an idealized 3x3 representation:\n         - For vertical patterns: Create a centered vertical line.\n         - For horizontal patterns: Create a centered horizontal line.\n         - For block patterns: Create a full or partial 3x3 block based on frequency.\n         - For corner patterns: Create an L-shape or complete it to form a square.\n         - For scattered patterns: Create a diagonal or symmetrical arrangement.\n      ii. Adjust the idealized shape to maintain key characteristics of the original shape.\n      iii. Ensure the idealized shape fits within a 3x3 grid while maximizing use of space.\n\n4. Horizontal Arrangement:\n   a. Sort idealized shapes based on their leftmost position in the original grid.\n   b. Place shapes in the output grid from left to right, maintaining relative ordering.\n   c. Adjust horizontal spacing to ensure shapes don't overlap and to optimize space usage.\n\n5. Vertical Alignment:\n   a. For each column in the output grid:\n      i. If a shape occupies only one or two rows, attempt to center it vertically.\n      ii. If multiple shapes share a column, adjust their vertical positions to avoid overlap and maximize symmetry.\n\n6. Pattern Completion:\n   a. Identify any partial patterns in the output grid (e.g., incomplete symmetries, partial lines).\n   b. Complete these patterns where possible, ensuring consistency with the original shape characteristics.\n\n7. Final Refinement:\n   a. Review the entire output grid for overall balance and symmetry.\n   b. Make minor adjustments to shape positions or details to enhance visual clarity and consistency.\n   c. Ensure that no colors overlap and that the grid uses space efficiently.\n\n8. Output Generation:\n   a. Create a new 3xN ColoredGrid object, where N is the width of the input grid.\n   b. Populate this grid with the refined, idealized shapes in their final positions.\n   c. Fill any remaining cells with the background color (0).\n\n9. Validation:\n   a. Verify that the output grid contains only 3 rows.\n   b. Check that all colors from the input grid are represented in the output.\n   c. Ensure that the relative horizontal ordering of shapes is maintained.\n\n10. Return the final ColoredGrid object as the solution.\n\nThis plan addresses the complexities identified in our reflection, including the nuanced approach to shape idealization, the preservation of essential characteristics, and the balance between individual shape representation and overall grid harmony. It should be flexible enough to handle various input patterns while consistently producing a coherent 3-row output that captures the essence of the input grid."
